crossover-wheel, the in-bottle probe.

probe_dinput.exe is a diagnostic, not part of the software. You do not need it
to use a wheel: the disk image on the releases page installs everything, and
this is for when something looks wrong and you want to know what DirectInput
itself sees inside the bottle.

It is separate from that image on purpose, because almost nobody needs it.

WHAT IT ANSWERS

Run it in the bottle with no arguments and it prints what DirectInput says the
device is: every axis and button with its offset, the axle number a game's
configuration records, the declared range, and whether any axis is marked as a
force feedback actuator. It then prints where each control sits in the state
struct a game reads, which is a different number from the enumeration offset
beside it and is the one that decides which value a game gets.

  wine --bottle "<name>" --no-gui --cx-app probe_dinput.exe

Give the name of the executable rather than a path to it. The wrapper then
searches the bottle for it and says so plainly if it is not there, instead of
handing an unchecked path to the loader. --no-gui puts any error from the
wrapper in the terminal rather than in a dialog box.

You will need to copy probe_dinput.exe into the bottle first, anywhere the
wrapper can find it, such as drive_c.

WHAT IT IS NOT FOR

"Does my game reach the wheel at all" has a cheaper answer that needs none of
this. Start the daemon with t150d -v, launch the game, and watch for:

  t150d: client connected from port 55437

No environment variable, no file path, nothing from a game launcher, so
nothing about a launcher's configuration can spoil it. That is what settled
Dakar Desert Rally: it steers the car and never connects, so it does not use
DirectInput at all. See docs/RESEARCH.md A48.

TWO EXTRA MODES

Add -i and it works through every control by name, one at a time: the wheel,
each pedal, both paddles, all thirteen buttons and the four hat directions.
For each it says which DirectInput object moved, and for a pedal it records
where it rests and how far it travels, so an inverted pedal is three numbers
rather than an impression. It asks you to confirm each identification, Enter
for yes, n if it named the wrong thing, s to skip a control this wheel does
not have, and it ends with a table of the whole wheel. Anything you disputed
is flagged in that table, and that is the line worth sending.

Add -f and it creates a real constant force and a real damper and plays them,
with no game involved, so a game that asks wrongly can be told apart from a
chain that does not deliver. Hold the wheel when you use -f.

WHERE THE OUTPUT GOES

Every run writes itself to a file under Z:\tmp, which is /tmp seen from macOS,
as well as to the screen: probe_dinput-dump.log for the plain run,
-controls.log for -i, -ffb.log for -f and -controls-ffb.log if you pass both
at once, so running all three leaves all three rather than each overwriting
the last. Send those files rather than a copied terminal: they are complete,
they survive the window being closed, and -o puts a run somewhere else if you
prefer.

MEASURING THE PROXY ITSELF

Run it again with --dll dinput8=b added to the wine command and it measures
CrossOver's builtin instead of the proxy. The difference between the two runs
is exactly what the proxy does.

It serves 64-bit bottles only: a 32-bit process is redirected to syswow64 and
never sees any of this.

docs/PROBES.md in the repository is the procedure and what each outcome means.
